{%- comment -%}
  ============================================================================
  Lookbook — decides which `lookbook` entries to show, and mounts the renderer
  that loads them through the Storefront API.

  This is the reusable entry point, so a lookbook can be dropped into any
  section, template or other snippet, not only sections/lookbook.liquid.

    {% render 'lookbook', uid: 'home-lookbook', heading: 'Shop the look' %}

  WHAT LIQUID DOES, AND WHAT IT LEAVES TO THE API
  Liquid writes the section's settings and the ids of the entries to show, in
  order. Everything about the looks themselves — titles, descriptions,
  templates, products and images — is loaded in the browser through the
  Storefront API by src/lookbook/storefront.js.

  Liquid keeps the choice of entries because only it can make it: a picked list
  is a section setting, admin order is not an order the API can sort by, and the
  API has no way to ask which entries feature a given product.

  Without a token in Theme settings > Storefront API there is nothing to load
  the looks with, so the snippet outputs nothing, and the theme editor says so.

  ACCEPTS (every one optional)
    uid                 unique id for the JSON script tag. Pass section.id from
                        a section. REQUIRED if two lookbooks share a page —
                        matching ids would make both mount the same payload.
    entry_source        'all' (default) for every Active entry, or 'selected' to
                        use the `entries` param.
    entries             a metaobject_list, used only when entry_source is
                        'selected'.
    entries_limit       looks shown at first. For every entry, Show more loads
                        the rest in batches of this size; on a product page it
                        caps the matches. Default 6.
    products_limit      max products per look, both sources. Default 12.
    heading             inline_richtext section heading. Omit for no heading.
    heading_size        'h0' | 'h1' | 'h2'. Default 'h1'.
    sub_heading         plain text under the section heading. Optional.
    description         richtext (already HTML) under the sub heading. Optional.
    show_sub_heading    show each ENTRY's sub heading. Default true.
    show_description    show each ENTRY's description. Default true.
    masonry_row_height  Masonry row unit in px; scales the card heights.
                        Default 120.
    product_cta_label   button text over each product image. NO default — omit
                        it, or pass an empty string, and no button is drawn.
    show_notices        render the theme-editor diagnostics. Default true.

  RELATING LOOKS TO ONE PRODUCT (used by sections/lookbook-related.liquid)
    containing_product  a product. When given, only entries that feature that
                        product are listed — through their picked products, or
                        the collection they pull from. The metaobject IS the
                        relationship, so no extra field is needed. Matches are
                        capped by `entries_limit` and load all at once.

  OUTPUT
  The JSON payload, and the mount point holding skeleton placeholders for the
  first looks (snippets/lookbook-skeleton.liquid) until React replaces them.

  Nothing at all when there is no token or no entry to show, apart from the
  theme-editor notices. A caller can capture the render and skip its own
  wrapper when the result is blank — both lookbook sections do, so an empty
  lookbook leaves no padded gap.

  WHICH PRODUCTS (per entry), for matching on a product page
  Each entry carries its own "Products Source" choice list. A value containing
  "collection" means its Collection field; anything else, including an empty
  choice, means its picked product list.

  FIELD KEYS are declared once in the {% liquid %} block below, for matching and
  the notices, and once in FIELD_KEYS in src/lookbook/storefront.js, for
  loading. Keys are not the labels shown in admin — expand a field in the
  metaobject definition to see its key. Renaming a label does NOT rename its
  key, which is why the products field is resolved from two candidate keys.

  Only entries with status Active appear. Draft entries are not exposed to the
  storefront, so they will not show here or on the live site.
  ============================================================================
{%- endcomment -%}

{%- liquid
  # ---- Field keys: must match FIELD_KEYS in src/lookbook/storefront.js ----
  assign key_title = 'title'
  assign key_subheading = 'sub_heading'
  assign key_description = 'discreption'
  assign key_template = 'template'
  assign key_collection = 'collection'
  assign key_products_source = 'products_source'

  # The product list field was created under an earlier label and later
  # relabelled "Select Products". Shopify keeps the original key on a rename, so
  # both are tried per entry; drop `key_products_legacy` once no definition
  # uses it any more.
  assign key_products = 'select_products'
  assign key_products_legacy = 'top_3_field'

  # ---- Parameter defaults ----
  # `allow_false: true` matters on the checkboxes: without it a deliberate
  # `false` from the caller would be treated as "not supplied" and flip to true.
  assign uid = uid | default: 'lookbook'
  assign entry_source = entry_source | default: 'all'
  assign entries_limit = entries_limit | default: 6
  assign products_limit = products_limit | default: 12
  assign heading_size = heading_size | default: 'h1'
  assign show_sub_heading = show_sub_heading | default: true, allow_false: true
  assign show_description = show_description | default: true, allow_false: true
  assign masonry_row_height = masonry_row_height | default: 120
  assign show_notices = show_notices | default: true, allow_false: true

  # ---- Storefront API ----
  # Shopify supports each Storefront API version for about a year, so move this
  # forward before it is retired.
  assign storefront_api_version = '2026-07'
  assign storefront_endpoint = 'https://' | append: shop.permanent_domain | append: '/api/' | append: storefront_api_version | append: '/graphql.json'
  assign storefront_token = settings.storefront_api_token | strip

  # ---- Which entries ----
  # A product page lists up to `entries_limit` matches. A picked list, or every
  # entry, lists all Liquid can see: Liquid loops over at most 50 entries, so
  # when every entry is listed and the cap is reached, `continue_paging` has the
  # renderer carry on through the API's own pages.
  if containing_product != blank
    assign lookbook_entries = shop.metaobjects.lookbook.values
    assign entry_limit = entries_limit
  elsif entry_source == 'selected'
    assign lookbook_entries = entries
    assign entry_limit = 50
  else
    assign lookbook_entries = shop.metaobjects.lookbook.values
    assign entry_limit = 50
  endif

  assign continue_paging = false
  if containing_product == blank and entry_source != 'selected' and lookbook_entries.size >= 50
    assign continue_paging = true
  endif

  # Counts ids actually emitted. An entry can be skipped (it may not feature
  # `containing_product`), so `forloop.last` does not mark the last one WRITTEN
  # and cannot drive the commas between them — a skipped final entry would leave
  # a trailing comma and break the JSON. The loop below emits a leading comma
  # from the second id onward instead.
  assign emitted_entries = 0
-%}

{%- capture entry_ids_json -%}
[
{%- for entry in lookbook_entries -%}
  {%- if emitted_entries >= entry_limit -%}{%- break -%}{%- endif -%}

  {%- liquid
    # Matched with `contains 'collection'` rather than an exact string so the
    # choice list values can read however the merchant wrote them ("Collection",
    # "From collection", ...).
    assign entry_source_value = entry[key_products_source].value | default: '' | downcase
    assign entry_collection = entry[key_collection].value

    if entry_source_value contains 'collection' and entry_collection == blank
      # The look has no products to show; the theme editor points at the entry.
      assign has_missing_collection = true
    endif

    # ---- Does this look feature `containing_product`? ----
    # With no product to relate to, every entry qualifies. Collection-sourced
    # looks are matched through the product's own collections rather than by
    # scanning the collection's products: that reads a list the product already
    # carries, and stays correct for collections larger than the 50 products
    # Liquid will hand back. A look set to Collection with no collection picked
    # has no products, so it features none — the products still in its picked
    # list must not match it.
    assign entry_matches = true
    if containing_product != blank
      assign entry_matches = false
      if entry_source_value contains 'collection'
        if entry_collection != blank
          for related_collection in containing_product.collections
            if related_collection.handle == entry_collection.handle
              assign entry_matches = true
              break
            endif
          endfor
        endif
      else
        assign entry_products = entry[key_products].value
        if entry_products == blank
          assign entry_products = entry[key_products_legacy].value
        endif
        for candidate in entry_products
          if candidate.id == containing_product.id
            assign entry_matches = true
            break
          endif
        endfor
      endif
    endif
  -%}

  {%- if entry_matches -%}
    {%- if emitted_entries > 0 -%},{%- endif -%}
    {%- assign emitted_entries = emitted_entries | plus: 1 -%}
    {{- 'gid://shopify/Metaobject/' | append: entry.system.id | json -}}
  {%- endif -%}
{%- endfor -%}
]
{%- endcapture -%}

{%- liquid
  # How many looks load at first. A picked list and a product page's matches
  # load whole; every entry loads `entries_limit` at a time, with Show more for
  # the rest.
  if containing_product != blank or entry_source == 'selected'
    assign page_size = emitted_entries
  else
    assign page_size = entries_limit
  endif

  assign render_lookbook = false
  if storefront_token != blank
    if emitted_entries > 0 or continue_paging
      assign render_lookbook = true
    endif
  endif
-%}

{%- capture payload -%}
{
  "heading": {{ heading | default: '' | json }},
  "headingSize": {{ heading_size | json }},
  "subHeading": {{ sub_heading | default: '' | json }},
  "description": {{ description | default: '' | json }},
  "showSubHeading": {{ show_sub_heading | json }},
  "showDescription": {{ show_description | json }},
  "masonryRowHeight": {{ masonry_row_height | json }},
  {%- comment -%}
    Passed through without a `default` filter on purpose: Liquid cannot tell an
    empty string from an unset param, so a default here would make an
    intentionally blank label in the theme editor spring back to a button.
  {%- endcomment -%}
  "productCtaLabel": {{ product_cta_label | default: '' | json }},
  {%- comment -%}
    Everything src/lookbook/storefront.js needs to load the looks. The token is
    a public Storefront API token, which is made to be read by browsers.
  {%- endcomment -%}
  "source": {
    "storefront": {
      "endpoint": {{ storefront_endpoint | json }},
      "token": {{ storefront_token | json }},
      "country": {{ localization.country.iso_code | json }},
      "language": {{ request.locale.iso_code | json }},
      "rootUrl": {{ routes.root_url | json }}
    },
    "entryIds": {{ entry_ids_json }},
    "pageSize": {{ page_size | json }},
    "productsLimit": {{ products_limit | json }},
    "continuePaging": {{ continue_paging | json }}
  },
  "labels": {
    "loadMore": {{ 'sections.lookbook.load_more' | t | json }},
    "loading": {{ 'accessibility.loading' | t | json }},
    "loadMoreError": {{ 'sections.lookbook.load_more_error' | t | json }},
    "loadError": {{ 'sections.lookbook.load_error' | t | json }}
  }
}
{%- endcapture -%}

{%- liquid
  # A closing script tag is neutralised in case a merchant pastes markup into
  # the heading or description settings — it would otherwise close the tag below
  # early and break the page.
  #
  # The needle is assembled from two halves so those characters never appear
  # literally in this file. Written out, Theme Check's HTML parser treats them
  # as closing the <script> element and fails with a LiquidHTMLSyntaxError,
  # which stops it parsing — and therefore checking — the rest of the snippet.
  #
  # The backslash below must survive into the output: `<\/script>` is a legal
  # JSON string escape that the HTML parser will not read as a closing tag.
  # Shopify's Liquid keeps it, because its lexer takes the raw text between the
  # quotes and never unescapes. Do not "tidy" this into a templating language
  # that does unescape string literals (liquidjs does) — there the two halves
  # collapse to the same value, `replace` becomes a no-op, and the guard is
  # silently gone.
  assign script_close = '</scr' | append: 'ipt>'
  assign script_close_safe = '<\/scr' | append: 'ipt>'
-%}
{%- liquid
  # Placeholders for the looks in the first batch, drawn from the first paint
  # until the Storefront API has returned them. Capped, so a long picked list
  # does not fill the page with placeholders.
  assign skeleton_looks = page_size | at_most: emitted_entries | at_most: 6
  assign skeleton_header = false
  if heading != blank or sub_heading != blank or description != blank
    assign skeleton_header = true
  endif
-%}
{%- comment -%}
  Nothing to load, no mount point: a blank result is what the OUTPUT contract in
  the header promises callers.
{%- endcomment -%}
{%- if render_lookbook -%}
<script type="application/json" id="LookbookData-{{ uid }}">
  {{- payload | replace: script_close, script_close_safe -}}
</script>

<div data-lookbook="LookbookData-{{ uid }}">
  {%- render 'lookbook-skeleton', looks: skeleton_looks, show_header: skeleton_header -%}
</div>
{%- endif -%}

{%- if request.design_mode and show_notices -%}
  {%- liquid
    # `.first` returns nothing on the metaobject values array, so probe the
    # first entry with a one-iteration loop instead. Getting this wrong made
    # the notice below fire even when the fields were resolving fine.
    assign fields_matched = false
    for probe_entry in lookbook_entries limit: 1
      if probe_entry[key_title] != nil or probe_entry[key_products] != nil or probe_entry[key_products_legacy] != nil or probe_entry[key_collection] != nil
        assign fields_matched = true
      endif
    endfor
  -%}
  {%- if storefront_token == blank -%}
    <div class="lookbook__notice page-width">
      <strong>Add a Storefront API token to show this lookbook.</strong>
      The looks are loaded through the Storefront API. In the Headless sales
      channel, give a storefront's public access token the metaobjects and
      product listings permissions, then paste it into
      Theme settings &gt; Storefront API. Until then the lookbook stays hidden
      on the storefront.
    </div>
  {%- elsif containing_product != blank and emitted_entries == 0 and lookbook_entries != blank -%}
    <div class="lookbook__notice page-width">
      <strong>No look features this product yet.</strong>
      This section shows the looks that already include the product being
      viewed, so nothing is shown until <em>{{ containing_product.title }}</em>
      is added to a lookbook entry — either to its picked products, or to the
      collection an entry points at. It stays hidden on the live storefront
      rather than rendering an empty heading.
    </div>
  {%- elsif lookbook_entries == blank and entry_source == 'selected' -%}
    <div class="lookbook__notice page-width">
      <strong>No entries picked yet.</strong>
      Use the Entries picker in this section to choose which looks to show
      and what order they appear in, or set Entries to show back to
      All entries.
    </div>
  {%- elsif lookbook_entries == blank -%}
    <div class="lookbook__notice page-width">
      <strong>No lookbook entries found.</strong>
      Check that the metaobject type handle is <code>lookbook</code>, that
      Storefronts API access is enabled on the definition, and that at least
      one entry has status Active rather than Draft.
    </div>
  {%- elsif fields_matched == false -%}
    <div class="lookbook__notice page-width">
      <strong>Found {{ lookbook_entries.size }} entries, but no fields matched.</strong>
      This snippet is looking for the field keys
      <code>{{ key_title }}</code>, <code>{{ key_subheading }}</code>,
      <code>{{ key_description }}</code>, <code>{{ key_template }}</code>,
      <code>{{ key_products }}</code> (or <code>{{ key_products_legacy }}</code>),
      <code>{{ key_collection }}</code> and <code>{{ key_products_source }}</code>.
      Open the metaobject definition, expand each field to see its real key,
      then update the keys at the top of <code>snippets/lookbook.liquid</code>
      and in <code>src/lookbook/storefront.js</code>.
    </div>
  {%- elsif has_missing_collection -%}
    <div class="lookbook__notice page-width">
      <strong>An entry is set to Collection but has no collection picked.</strong>
      Open that entry in Settings &gt; Custom data and either choose a
      collection, or set its Products Source back to the picked-products
      option.
    </div>
  {%- endif -%}
{%- endif -%}
